{"id":30,"course_id":8,"title":"Network Policies and Tenant Isolation","summary":"Control pod-to-pod traffic deliberately instead of trusting flat networking.","sequence":2,"body":"## Network policy model\nNetworkPolicy resources define allowed ingress and egress for selected Pods, but enforcement depends on a network plugin that supports policies.\n- Start with namespace or app default-deny policies.\n- Allow only required traffic: ingress controller, dependencies, DNS, metrics, and health checks.\n- Test egress rules carefully; blocking DNS can look like many unrelated failures.\n- Document ownership of shared services.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl get networkpolicy -A\n$ kubectl describe networkpolicy -n payments\n$ kubectl get pods -n payments --show-labels\n$ kubectl exec -n payments deploy/checkout -- nslookup kubernetes.default\nLook for policies selecting no Pods, labels that do not match, missing DNS egress, and shared namespaces without boundaries.\n\n## Tradeoff\nDefault-deny improves containment, but rollout without dependency inventory can cause outages.","practice_notes":"Scenario: After default-deny, apps cannot call DNS.\nChecklist:\n- Confirm the policy selects the Pods.\n- Add explicit egress to kube-dns/CoreDNS.\n- Test dependency traffic one service at a time.\n- Keep a rollback manifest for the policy rollout.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get networkpolicy -n payments -o yaml\n$ kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=kube-dns -o wide","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-kubernetes-security-multitenancy","course_category":"Security","course_era":"Platform Academy","terms":[{"id":146,"term":"NetworkPolicy","context":"Traffic policy","definition":"A Kubernetes resource controlling allowed Pod ingress and egress."},{"id":147,"term":"Default deny","context":"Isolation","definition":"A policy posture that blocks traffic unless explicitly allowed."},{"id":148,"term":"Ingress","context":"Incoming traffic","definition":"Traffic entering selected Pods."},{"id":149,"term":"Egress","context":"Outgoing traffic","definition":"Traffic leaving selected Pods."},{"id":150,"term":"Policy enforcement","context":"CNI support","definition":"Whether the installed network plugin enforces NetworkPolicy."}],"flashcards":[{"id":88,"lesson_id":30,"prompt":"What must exist for NetworkPolicy to work?","answer":"A network plugin that enforces NetworkPolicy.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":89,"lesson_id":30,"prompt":"Why can blocking DNS be confusing?","answer":"Many dependencies fail by name and appear unrelated.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":90,"lesson_id":30,"prompt":"What is the rollout risk of default-deny?","answer":"Missing dependency inventory can break service traffic.","hint":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
